Hello,
I found another bug, this time it seems to be related to a non-terminating loop in a program with recursion:
```
void recursive(int n) {
  if (n == 0)
    while (n == 0)
      ;
  else 
    recursive(0);
}

void call_recursive() { recursive(0); }

int main(void) {
  call_recursive();
  return 0;
}
```
Here symbiotic outputs erroneously  when invoked with `--prp=termination`:
```
...
No error found.
RESULT: true
```
I also managed to confirm that this is not fixed by the fix for my recent report https://github.com/staticafi/symbiotic/issues/274

Hello everyone, 
I found another bug but this time symbiotic's termination analysis returns erroneously `RESULT: true` 
```
void test() {
  int counter = 0;
  while (counter == 0)
    ;
}

void runTest() {
  test();
}

int main() {
  runTest();
  return 0;
}
```
I managed to checkout the recent fix for https://github.com/staticafi/symbiotic/issues/268 and it seems the bug remains.

Hello everyone, I just played a bit with symbiotic from last SV-Comp and found that it returns erroneously `RESULT: false(termination)` for

```
extern void __VERIFIER_assume(int cond);

int one(){
  int v1 = 1;
  if (v1 < 0)
    return 1;
  else
    return 1;
}

void h() {
  while(one() <= 0) {
  }
}
void f() {
  h();
}

int main() {
  f();
  __VERIFIER_assume(0);
  return 0;
}
```
The condition of the while loop should evaluate to false and the program should always terminate. Crucially, the assumption here makes the difference: Removing it makes symbiotic detect termination. I used the commands from SV-Comp:
`symbiotic/bin/symbiotic --witness witness.graphml --sv-comp --prp=termination.prp --64 test.c`

So is this actually related to the assumption?
Cheers!

