Assume that we are on system that conforms System V AMD64 ABI. The problem is that C99 says that

> An object declared as type _Bool is large enough to store the values 0 and 1.

However, on the assumed platform it holds that `1B == sizeof(char) == sizeof(_Bool)`. Therefore, there are some bit patterns that do not represent valid values of the given type and reading _trap representations_ by any other means than through pointer to a character type is UB.

This means that `clang` will emit IR where the variable of type `_Bool` is represented by an `alloca` of type `i8`. So `klee_make_symbolic(&bool, sizeof(_Bool), "bool")` will also, unfortunately, produce bit patterns that are not valid:

```c
#include <klee/klee.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    _Bool b;
    klee_make_symbolic(&b, sizeof(_Bool), "b");
    if (*(unsigned char*)&b == true)
        puts("b == true");
    else if (*(unsigned char*)&b == false)
        puts("b == false");
    else
        puts("wtf?");
}
```
